### Why is a plain append-only table not tamper-evident?

An append-only table is one where you revoke UPDATE and DELETE permissions from the application role. That stops the ordinary application from changing history, but a database administrator, a superuser, or someone with direct storage access can still alter or delete rows, and the data looks perfectly normal afterward. Append-only controls who should change data; it does not detect that a change happened.

```sql
-- Append-only table: revoke UPDATE/DELETE
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
    seq_no      BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    event_type  VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    actor       VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    payload     JSONB NOT NULL,
    created_at  TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW()
);

REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE ON audit_log FROM app_role;
-- A DBA can still: UPDATE audit_log SET payload = '...' WHERE seq_no = 42;
-- Nothing in the table reveals this happened.
```

### How does the hash chain make tampering detectable?

Each record stores a hash computed over its own content plus the previous record's hash. Changing any record changes its hash, which no longer matches what the next record hashed over, breaking every later link.

```java
package com.bank.audit;

import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.HexFormat;

public class HashChainBuilder {

    public static String computeHash(long seqNo, String eventType, String actor,
                                      String payload, String previousHash) {
        try {
            String content = seqNo + "|" + eventType + "|" + actor + "|" 
                + payload + "|" + previousHash;
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
            byte[] digest = md.digest(content.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
            return HexFormat.of().formatHex(digest);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Hash computation failed", e);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String hash0 = "0000000000000000"; // genesis
        String hash1 = computeHash(1, "LOGIN", "Rajesh", "{}", hash0);
        String hash2 = computeHash(2, "TRANSFER", "Rajesh", 
            "{\"amount\":50000}", hash1);
        System.out.println("Record 1 hash: " + hash1);
        System.out.println("Record 2 hash: " + hash2);
        // If record 1 is altered, recomputing hash1 gives a different value,
        // which does not match the previousHash stored in record 2.
    }
}
```

```text
Record 1 hash: a3f2b8c1...  (depends on content + genesis)
Record 2 hash: 7e4d9a0f...  (depends on content + hash of record 1)
# Changing record 1 -> new hash1' -> record 2's previousHash mismatch -> TAMPER DETECTED
```

### How do you verify the chain?

Walk from a known-good starting point, recomputing each hash and comparing. The first mismatch pinpoints where tampering occurred.

```java
package com.bank.audit;

import java.util.List;

public class ChainVerifier {

    public boolean verify(List<AuditRecord> records) {
        String expectedPrevHash = "0000000000000000"; // genesis
        for (AuditRecord record : records) {
            if (!record.getPreviousHash().equals(expectedPrevHash)) {
                System.err.println("TAMPER DETECTED at seq " + record.getSeqNo()
                    + ": expected prev=" + expectedPrevHash
                    + " but found prev=" + record.getPreviousHash());
                return false;
            }
            String recomputed = HashChainBuilder.computeHash(
                record.getSeqNo(), record.getEventType(), record.getActor(),
                record.getPayload(), record.getPreviousHash());
            if (!recomputed.equals(record.getHash())) {
                System.err.println("TAMPER DETECTED at seq " + record.getSeqNo()
                    + ": hash mismatch");
                return false;
            }
            expectedPrevHash = record.getHash();
        }
        System.out.println("Chain verified: all " + records.size() + " records intact.");
        return true;
    }
}
```

### How do signed checkpoints catch whole-chain rewrites?

If an attacker has full write access, they could recompute every hash to produce a consistent new chain. Periodically signing the chain head with a private key (stored in an HSM) and saving the signed checkpoint to WORM storage prevents this.

```java
package com.bank.audit;

import java.security.*;
import java.util.Base64;

public class CheckpointSigner {

    private final PrivateKey privateKey; // from HSM

    public CheckpointSigner(PrivateKey privateKey) {
        this.privateKey = privateKey;
    }

    public String signChainHead(String chainHeadHash) throws Exception {
        Signature sig = Signature.getInstance("SHA256withRSA");
        sig.initSign(privateKey);
        sig.update(chainHeadHash.getBytes());
        return Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(sig.sign());
    }

    // Store signed checkpoint in WORM storage.
    // A rewritten chain's head won't match the previously signed checkpoint.
}
```

### How do you tier data for 7-year retention?

Recent data (last 90 days) stays in a fast, indexed, queryable store. Older data moves to cheap cold object storage with object-lock immutability, partitioned by day.

```java
package com.bank.audit;

import java.time.LocalDate;

public class StorageTieringPolicy {

    public String determineStorage(LocalDate eventDate) {
        long daysOld = java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(
            eventDate, LocalDate.now());
        if (daysOld <= 90) {
            return "HOT: PostgreSQL (fast, indexed, queryable)";
        } else if (daysOld <= 365) {
            return "WARM: S3 Standard (compressed, indexed)";
        } else {
            return "COLD: S3 Glacier (object-lock, 7-year retention)";
        }
    }
}
```

### How do you keep cold data queryable?

A lightweight index maps query keys (time, actor, entity) to the object and offset in cold storage.

```java
package com.bank.audit;

public class ColdStorageIndex {

    // Index entry: actor=Rajesh, date=2023-01-15 -> s3://audit-cold/2023/01/15/chunk-42.gz, offset=1024
    public String findInColdStorage(String actor, String date) {
        // Look up in the lightweight index (Elasticsearch or metadata table)
        return "s3://audit-cold/" + date.replace("-", "/") + "/chunk-42.gz";
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is assuming append-only is sufficient for tamper-evidence. The second is signing the chain head with a key stored in the same database, which means an attacker who owns the DB also owns the key.

```java
package com.bank.audit.antipattern;

// WRONG: signing key stored in the same DB as the audit log
// If the attacker owns the DB, they own the key and can forge checkpoints.

// CORRECT: signing key in an HSM (Hardware Security Module)
// The attacker cannot extract the key even with DB access.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How does this compare to blockchain? What is the performance overhead of hashing every record? How do you handle the case where the hash chain verifier itself is compromised? How would you implement this with Amazon QLDB? What is the difference between tamper-evident and tamper-proof?
